DESIGN A (STATIC ROW-SPLIT FOR TILE RECONSTRUCTION) — SCOUTED, BLOCKED, AND THE
LARGEST SOUND SUBSET LANDED AND MEASURED (issue #455)
================================================================================

Host      Apple M4 Pro, 8 P + 4 E cores (12 logical), Darwin 25.5.0. Idle box.
Base      main ee07b00 (the #467 merge). NOT 8568dbb — see section 1.
Branch    perf/recon-rowsplit @ a996a57.
Build     cargo build --release, default features, NO -C target-cpu=native.
Timing    NO `nice` on any timed run. Two-point wall fit (2 and 20 frames,
          beta = (hi - lo) / 18) so process startup drops out.
dav1d     1.5.4 at --framedelay 1, measured in the SAME interleaved sweep.
Files     recon_rowsplit_gap_2026-08-09.tsv    216 rows, 9 complete rounds
          recon_rowsplit_sites_2026-08-09.tsv  registration census, base + head,
                                               t=1 and t=8, 68 sites each


0. WHAT WAS ASKED, AND WHAT THE DATA CHOSE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The site-class round (perf/site-class, #455 comment) priced the borrow tracker
by call-site class to decide between two designs:

  A  static row-split of the picture buffer at tile setup, removing the tracker
     from tile RECONSTRUCTION. `chunks_mut(stride)` yields provably disjoint
     rows, `split_at_mut` at tile column boundaries yields provably disjoint
     per-tile segments — safe Rust, no `unsafe`, computed once.
  B  a non-atomic serial tracker behind the monotone parallelism latch. t=1 only.

Its verdict, against a rule pre-registered at 2b5e41f: **A**, because A's share
of the excess over the 1.30x bar HOLDS across thread counts (33-58% at every
t > 1 cell) while B is structurally t=1-only. That reasoning was re-derived from
its raw 480 rows here and reproduces exactly (cls_recon+big deltas 10.6 / 12.2 /
12.4 / 7.3 ms at 8bpc t=1/2/4/8; 13.3 / 15.8 / 15.3 / 12.1 at 10bpc).

This round built A. The result is in three parts, worst first.


1. DESIGN A AS SPECIFIED CANNOT BE WRITTEN IN SAFE RUST IN THIS ARCHITECTURE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The design's premise is that a `&mut` to the picture plane exists at tile setup
to split. **It does not, anywhere, at any thread count.** Traced in source:

  include/dav1d/picture.rs:1287  `Rav1dPicture::data: Option<Arc<Rav1dPictureData>>`
                                 — the planes live behind an `Arc` that is
                                 shared with `sr_cur`, the reference-frame slots
                                 and the output queue while the frame decodes.
  src/internal.rs:803            `Rav1dFrameContext::data: RwLock<Rav1dFrameData>`
  src/thread_task.rs:1237        the tile task opens `fc.data.try_read()` and
        (and :1416, :1436, :1451) calls `rav1d_decode_tile_sbrow(c, &mut tc, &f)`
                                 with `&Rav1dFrameData`. Every filter task does
                                 the same. Read guards are shared — that IS the
                                 tile parallelism — so no task ever holds `&mut`.
  src/lib.rs:221-225             workers are `'static` threads spawned once at
                                 context creation, pulling from a global task
                                 queue. No scoped-thread lifetime can carry a
                                 borrow of a frame's buffer into them, and the
                                 queue is shared across frames by design.

So the only route from `&DisjointMut<PicBuf>` to `&mut [Pixel]` rows is the one
`DisjointMut` already provides — a runtime-checked guard. A "static split" here
would mean handing out `&mut` on a hand-written invariant instead of a compiler
proof, i.e. writing the `unsafe` the task's soundness section says to STOP on.
Per that instruction: STOPPED, not written.

Note this is upstream of the obstacle the task expected. The expected hard part
was recon's access pattern (overlapping simultaneous views). That part is
actually FINE — `ipred_prepare` materialises the neighbour edge into a scratch
array first, so the prediction kernels are pure block writers, and intra reads
provably do not cross a tile column (`src/ipred_prepare.rs:127-131`, `:229`).
The blocker is ownership, not aliasing.

The measurement round checked the access-pattern premise in source and reported
it holding. It did not check the `&mut`-availability premise. That is the gap
between "A is the right target" and "A is buildable".


2. A'S REALIZABLE MECHANISM ALREADY LANDED, 44 MINUTES AFTER THE DECISION'S BASE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
There IS a form of A that works: take ONE tracker registration over the strided
hull, then let borrowck split THAT into rows. The `&mut` comes from a guard
instead of from tile setup; the row-disjointness proof is still the compiler's.

That is exactly `for_rows` / `for_rows_mut`, merged as #467 (ee07b00,
2026-08-08 16:44) — after the site-class base 8568dbb. It is already applied to
every top `recon+big` site the decision was computed on (`ipred_arm.rs` cfl_pred,
`ipred.rs` splat_dc / z1 / z2 / paeth / smooth*, cdef read+write). Registrations
per frame at 8bpc t=1 went 15,646,727 -> 7,924,706.

**And it is gated off above one thread, by construction and necessarily.** The
hull reserves the inter-row gaps; AV1 tiles partition a frame by COLUMNS, so a
neighbouring tile's legitimate write lands in a reserved gap and the tracker
reports a false overlap (#467 measured 8-9 spurious panics in 24 concurrent runs
before the latch). Verified here: the t=8 census is 22,700,725 registrations on
BOTH 8568dbb and ee07b00 — bit-for-bit unmoved.

Consequence for the decision: **the property A was chosen for — a share that
holds across thread counts — is not available to any realizable form of A in
this architecture.** Every form that can be written is t=1-only, which is B's
coverage. The choice between A and B was sound on the numbers and is moot on the
architecture.


3. WHAT THIS ROUND BUILT: THE ONE PICTURE SITE #467 COULD NOT CONVERT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#467 §5c left exactly one `recon+big` site undone, and left it for the reason
the task named as the hard part: `cfl_ac` needs a luma row AND the row below it
LIVE AT ONCE (4:2:0 chroma AC averages a row pair), and `for_rows`'s callback
owns a row's guard for exactly its own duration.

`Rav1dPictureDataComponentOffset::for_row_pairs` adds that one degree of freedom
— `n` logical rows at `step`, each optionally with the picture row beneath it —
under the SAME hull-vs-per-row policy, with no copy in either branch. Both
borrows are immutable, so the pair cannot conflict with itself: two subslices of
one guard in the hull branch, two live guards in the per-row branch.

Registrations per frame, `v4k_8tile` 8bpc, `--features probe-sites`:

  cell   site (cfl_ac)              base        head       change
  t=1    ipred_arm.rs cfl_ac      629,080      67,862     -89.2%
         mean extent                10.2 B   31,766 B     (the hull)
         FRAME TOTAL            7,924,706   7,363,488      -7.1%
  t=8    ipred_arm.rs cfl_ac      629,080     629,080       0.0%
         FRAME TOTAL           22,700,725  22,700,725       0.0%


4. THE WALL-CLOCK RESULT IS A NULL, AT EVERY CELL
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
9 complete rounds, 216 rows, 72 cells committed, 2 discarded and re-run for
load, **ZERO rows under foreign load**. ms/frame, median [min..max] over n=9:

  cell                base                    head                    dav1d   delta   disjoint
  v4k_8tile t=1       319.2 [317.1..320.9]    318.1 [315.7..320.7]    246.5  -0.35%   no
  v4k_8tile t=2       198.3 [197.3..198.7]    197.9 [197.3..199.8]    125.1  -0.17%   no
  v4k_8tile t=4       107.3 [106.7..108.9]    106.9 [106.3..108.3]     65.6  -0.36%   no
  v4k_8tile t=8        68.2 [ 65.1.. 68.8]     68.2 [ 65.0.. 69.3]     36.3  +0.08%   no
  v4k_8tile_10b t=1   359.1 [356.9..361.1]    356.8 [355.3..360.1]    251.2  -0.63%   no
  v4k_8tile_10b t=2   206.2 [205.5..207.9]    206.4 [204.6..207.2]    127.6  +0.11%   no
  v4k_8tile_10b t=4   111.5 [110.4..114.1]    111.1 [110.3..113.4]     66.8  -0.40%   no
  v4k_8tile_10b t=8    72.9 [ 69.5.. 75.2]     72.4 [ 69.3.. 73.9]     37.6  -0.69%   no

  base/dav1d  1.295 1.585 1.635 1.879 (8bpc) | 1.429 1.617 1.668 1.941 (10bpc)
  head/dav1d  1.291 1.582 1.629 1.881       | 1.420 1.618 1.662 1.928

**DISJOINT AT 0 OF 8 CELLS.** Every head band overlaps its base band.

Paired within-round (the statistic the interleaved rotation exists for), median
of (head - base) per round with an exact two-sided sign test:

  cell                median      %       sign(h<b)   p
  v4k_8tile t=1        -1.33   -0.42%      6/9      0.508
  v4k_8tile t=2        +0.17   +0.08%      3/9      0.508
  v4k_8tile t=4        -0.28   -0.26%      8/9      0.039   <- see below
  v4k_8tile t=8        -0.06   -0.08%      5/9      1.000
  v4k_8tile_10b t=1    -2.17   -0.60%      6/9      0.508
  v4k_8tile_10b t=2    +0.33   +0.16%      4/9      1.000
  v4k_8tile_10b t=4    -0.06   -0.05%      5/9      1.000
  v4k_8tile_10b t=8    +0.06   +0.08%      4/9      1.000

**The t > 1 cells are a free NEGATIVE CONTROL**: the census proves the head
registers byte-identical extents there, so any reading is noise by construction.
One of them (8bpc t=4) comes back at p = 0.039. That is the harness's
false-positive rate at n = 9 over 8 tests, and it is LARGER than every t=1
reading's significance. So the t=1 medians (-0.42% / -0.60%, p = 0.508) are not
distinguishable from that noise floor.

Honest bound, both directions. The site-class rate for `recon+big` at 8bpc t=1
is 10.6 ms over 4,309,973 registrations; the 561,218 removed here predict
~1.4 ms (0.44%). The observed -1.33 ms paired median is the same order as that
prediction — but the run-to-run spread is 4-5 ms, so this sweep **cannot resolve
it**. This is "not resolvable at n=9", NOT "proven zero".

This is the third time this campaign that cutting registration COUNT has failed
to move wall clock (AGENT_BRIEF §6: `TinyLock`, `block_mut` held row guards).
The count reduction here is real, verified and large (-89.2% at the site, -7.1%
of the frame); the wall clock did not follow.


